1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:05,486
What if you bought five thousand dollars of stuff on a credit card, and only ever paid the minimum?

2
00:00:05,806 --> 00:00:10,891
No missed payments. No late fees. You do exactly what the bill asks you to do.

3
00:00:11,211 --> 00:00:13,139
It would take you nineteen years to pay it off.

4
00:00:13,459 --> 00:00:18,486
And that five thousand dollars of stuff would end up costing you almost thirteen thousand.

5
00:00:18,806 --> 00:00:24,762
Today, two versions of you get the same card, the same bill, and make one tiny different choice.

6
00:00:25,562 --> 00:00:29,195
It starts on a good day. You just got the card, and Max has ideas.

7
00:00:29,515 --> 00:00:36,957
New phone, because yours has a crack. A sofa, because you're an adult now. A weekend trip, because life is short.

8
00:00:37,277 --> 00:00:39,681
And a few gadgets, because Max said so.

9
00:00:40,001 --> 00:00:45,527
Swipe. Swipe. Swipe. It doesn't even feel like money. It feels like tapping a piece of plastic.

10
00:00:45,847 --> 00:00:47,902
Total: five thousand dollars.

11
00:00:48,702 --> 00:00:51,001
A month later, the bill arrives.

12
00:00:51,321 --> 00:00:58,089
Balance: five thousand dollars. Minimum payment due: one hundred and thirty eight dollars and twenty nine cents.

13
00:00:58,409 --> 00:01:00,209
You breathe out. That's it? That's easy.

14
00:01:00,529 --> 00:01:05,881
But look closer. Of those one hundred and thirty eight dollars, eighty eight dollars are interest.

15
00:01:06,201 --> 00:01:09,115
Only fifty dollars actually go toward the stuff you bought.

16
00:01:09,435 --> 00:01:14,346
So every month, most of your payment just pays for the privilege of still owing money.

17
00:01:15,146 --> 00:01:18,072
Where does that interest come from? The annual percentage rate.

18
00:01:18,392 --> 00:01:26,949
In August twenty twenty six, the average credit card rate in America, on accounts that pay interest, was twenty one point one nine percent.

19
00:01:27,269 --> 00:01:31,947
Ten years earlier, in twenty sixteen, it was about twelve and a half.

20
00:01:32,267 --> 00:01:37,004
So the same card balance is now much more expensive to carry than it used to be.

21
00:01:37,804 --> 00:01:39,499
Now the timeline splits.

22
00:01:39,819 --> 00:01:45,160
Timeline A pays exactly the minimum, every month, forever. Never late. Never more.

23
00:01:45,480 --> 00:01:50,275
Timeline B decides to pay a flat two hundred dollars every month until it's gone.

24
00:01:50,595 --> 00:01:54,751
Neither of them buys anything else on the card. Same balance, same rate.

25
00:01:55,071 --> 00:01:57,950
Just sixty two dollars a month of difference at the start.

26
00:01:58,750 --> 00:02:00,480
Timeline A. One year later.

27
00:02:00,800 --> 00:02:05,212
You've made twelve perfect payments. About one thousand six hundred dollars, gone.

28
00:02:05,532 --> 00:02:09,294
You check the balance. Four thousand four hundred and thirty two dollars.

29
00:02:09,614 --> 00:02:13,851
A whole year of paying, and the balance dropped by less than six hundred.

30
00:02:14,171 --> 00:02:19,257
Max says it's normal. Max also still owes you twenty dollars from two thousand nineteen.

31
00:02:20,057 --> 00:02:23,435
Year five. The sofa has a stain shaped like Australia.

32
00:02:23,755 --> 00:02:26,507
The phone is so old it's no longer getting updates.

33
00:02:26,827 --> 00:02:30,461
You still owe two thousand seven hundred and thirty six dollars on them.

34
00:02:30,781 --> 00:02:33,613
You're paying for a sofa you would now pay someone to take away.

35
00:02:34,413 --> 00:02:39,557
Somewhere around year three, timeline A gets a happy email.

36
00:02:39,877 --> 00:02:43,534
Congratulations! You've been approved for a higher credit limit.

37
00:02:43,854 --> 00:02:52,399
It feels like a reward. Like the bank trusts you.

38
00:02:52,719 --> 00:02:56,794
But think about what the bank sees. A customer who always pays, never pays it all, and keeps a balance month after month.

39
00:02:57,114 --> 00:03:01,351
To a card company, that's not a problem customer. That's the perfect customer.

40
00:03:01,671 --> 00:03:05,201
Max, of course, immediately suggests a bigger TV.

41
00:03:06,001 --> 00:03:09,948
Year ten. You've moved apartments twice. The sofa is gone. The phone is in a drawer.

42
00:03:10,268 --> 00:03:11,905
Balance: one thousand four hundred and ninety seven dollars.

43
00:03:12,225 --> 00:03:19,029
You have now paid nine thousand six hundred and eighty nine dollars in total. Almost double the price of everything. And you still owe almost fifteen hundred.

44
00:03:19,349 --> 00:03:23,598
And the payments are getting smaller, so the end keeps moving further away.

45
00:03:24,398 --> 00:03:28,102
Timeline A finally pays it off after two hundred and twenty nine months.

46
00:03:28,422 --> 00:03:32,450
Nineteen years and one month.

47
00:03:32,770 --> 00:03:40,305
Total paid: twelve thousand seven hundred and eighty two dollars. For five thousand dollars of stuff that's long gone.

48
00:03:40,625 --> 00:03:44,874
Seven thousand seven hundred and eighty two dollars of that was pure interest.

49
00:03:45,194 --> 00:03:50,163
You could have bought the whole spree again. And again. With the interest alone.

50
00:03:50,963 --> 00:03:53,309
Now timeline B. Two hundred dollars a month, no matter what.

51
00:03:53,629 --> 00:03:57,785
Thirty four months later, the card is at zero. Less than three years.

52
00:03:58,105 --> 00:04:02,018
Total paid: six thousand six hundred and fifty five dollars. Interest: one thousand six hundred and fifty five.

53
00:04:02,338 --> 00:04:05,391
Still not free. But six thousand one hundred dollars cheaper than timeline A.

54
00:04:05,711 --> 00:04:10,773
And for the next sixteen years, that two hundred dollars a month is free to do something else.

55
00:04:11,573 --> 00:04:13,767
And this is where the gap gets really big.

56
00:04:14,087 --> 00:04:19,416
After month thirty four, timeline B still has the habit of paying two hundred dollars a month. It just sends it somewhere else now.

57
00:04:19,736 --> 00:04:24,148
Remember our last video? Two hundred dollars a month invested in an S and P five hundred index fund from twenty ten to twenty twenty six turned forty thousand two hundred dollars into about one hundred and thirty thousand.

58
00:04:24,468 --> 00:04:28,915
Past results, not a promise. But that's the size of what's at stake.

59
00:04:29,235 --> 00:04:33,554
Timeline A spends those same years paying interest on a sofa that's already in a landfill.

60
00:04:34,354 --> 00:04:36,095
Here are both balances side by side.

61
00:04:36,415 --> 00:04:42,197
Timeline B drops like a rock and hits zero before year three.

62
00:04:42,517 --> 00:04:46,487
Timeline A slides down so slowly that it almost looks flat.

63
00:04:46,807 --> 00:04:53,460
That long tail is where the bank makes most of its money from you.

64
00:04:54,260 --> 00:04:58,161
There's also a cost that doesn't show up on the statement.

65
00:04:58,481 --> 00:05:03,613
Carrying debt for years means every unexpected bill hits harder. A broken laptop, a car repair, a dentist visit.

66
00:05:03,933 --> 00:05:07,090
And when there's no cushion, those surprises often go right back on the same card.

67
00:05:07,410 --> 00:05:12,716
That's how a five thousand dollar balance quietly becomes a lifestyle.

68
00:05:13,516 --> 00:05:15,803
And look what happens when you pay even more.

69
00:05:16,123 --> 00:05:21,139
One hundred and fifty dollars a month: about four years, two thousand six hundred in interest.

70
00:05:21,459 --> 00:05:24,942
Three hundred a month: twenty months, under one thousand dollars of interest.

71
00:05:25,262 --> 00:05:29,917
Five hundred a month: one year, five hundred and fifty dollars of interest.

72
00:05:30,237 --> 00:05:34,417
Every extra dollar you pay today kills several dollars of future interest.

73
00:05:35,217 --> 00:05:38,166
So why is the minimum so low?

74
00:05:38,486 --> 00:05:44,593
The minimum is the smallest payment the bank will accept without calling you late. It's not a recommendation.

75
00:05:44,913 --> 00:05:50,335
It's designed to keep your account in good standing. Not to get you out of debt.

76
00:05:50,655 --> 00:05:57,284
In the US, your statement must even show how long paying only the minimum would take. Most people never read that box.

77
00:05:58,084 --> 00:06:04,783
If you're already in timeline A, here's how people usually get out.

78
00:06:05,103 --> 00:06:09,317
One. Stop adding new purchases to the card. You can't bail out a boat that's still taking water.

79
00:06:09,637 --> 00:06:11,263
Two. Pay a fixed amount, above the minimum, and automate it.

80
00:06:11,583 --> 00:06:16,703
Three. If you have several cards, put every extra dollar on the one with the highest rate first.

81
00:06:17,023 --> 00:06:24,360
Four. Call and ask for a lower rate, or look at a balance transfer offer. Read the fees and the end date carefully.

82
00:06:24,680 --> 00:06:30,636
And five. Build even a small emergency fund, so the next surprise doesn't land on the card.

83
00:06:31,436 --> 00:06:33,747
And the same idea now hides behind friendlier buttons.

84
00:06:34,067 --> 00:06:39,268
Pay in four. Split it. Only twenty five dollars today.

85
00:06:39,588 --> 00:06:42,630
Some of these plans charge no interest if every payment is on time. Others charge fees, or work like a loan.

86
00:06:42,950 --> 00:06:48,151
The danger isn't one plan. It's ten small plans at once, each looking harmless on its own.

87
00:06:48,471 --> 00:06:54,497
Same rule as the card. Know the total, know the end date, and never let the small number hide the big one.

88
00:06:55,297 --> 00:06:58,338
To be fair, credit cards aren't evil.

89
00:06:58,658 --> 00:07:04,707
If you pay the full statement balance every month, many cards charge you no interest at all.

90
00:07:05,027 --> 00:07:10,577
The card is a tool. The minimum payment is the trap.

91
00:07:11,377 --> 00:07:18,064
The honest part: this is not financial advice.

92
00:07:18,384 --> 00:07:22,529
Card terms, minimum payment formulas and rates differ by bank and by country. Check your own statement.

93
00:07:22,849 --> 00:07:24,544
If debt feels overwhelming, non-profit credit counselling services can help you make a plan.

94
00:07:25,344 --> 00:07:31,996
Same card. Same five thousand dollars. One version of you pays for nineteen years. The other is free in under three.

95
00:07:32,316 --> 00:07:36,357
Have you ever been stuck paying the minimum? Tell me in the comments, no judgement.

96
00:07:36,677 --> 00:07:38,337
And I'll see you in the other timeline.

